.TH START-CLAUDE 1 "2026-10-21" "Noble Factor" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
Start-Claude \- open a tmux workspace onto the Claude Code conversation of a
schedule, or of a conversation it names, that claude runs in its background
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B Start-Claude
.RB [ \-C | \-\-directory
.IR DIRECTORY ]
.RB [ \-w | \-\-shell\-width\-percent
.IR PERCENT ]
.RB [ \-d | \-\-detach | \-\-no\-remote\-control ]
.BR \-s | \-\-schedule
.I SCHEDULE
.RB [ \-r | \-\-resume
.IR CONVERSATION ]
.RI [ \-\- " claude-argument" ...]
.br
.B Start-Claude
.RB [ \-C | \-\-directory
.IR DIRECTORY ]
.RB [ \-w | \-\-shell\-width\-percent
.IR PERCENT ]
.RB [ \-d | \-\-detach | \-\-no\-remote\-control ]
.BR \-r | \-\-resume
.I CONVERSATION
.RI [ \-\- " claude-argument" ...]
.br
.B Start-Claude
.RB [ \-C | \-\-directory
.IR DIRECTORY ]
.RB [ \-w | \-\-shell\-width\-percent
.IR PERCENT ]
.RB [ \-d | \-\-detach | \-\-no\-remote\-control ]
.BR \-c | \-\-continue
.RB [ \-s | \-\-schedule
.IR SCHEDULE ]
.RI [ \-\- " claude-argument" ...]
.br
.B Start-Claude
.BR \-h | \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Start-Claude
opens a
.BR tmux (1)
session onto a Claude Code conversation that claude runs in its background, as
.B "claude \-\-bg"
starts one: the conversation's viewer,
.BR "claude attach" ,
in the left pane, and your shell in the right, then attaches. The left pane
holds keyboard focus on attach. With
.B \-\-detach
it opens no tmux session; see
.BR "STARTING AND ATTACHING" .
.PP
It prints the background session's short id on standard output, the id
.B "claude \-\-bg"
prints and
.B "claude attach"
takes: with
.B \-\-detach
at once, and otherwise when the tmux session's client leaves.
Everything else it says, and tmux's own words, go to standard error.
.PP
Each start opens a tmux session of its own. Two starts of one schedule, from two
terminals or two devices, open two tmux sessions onto the same conversation, and
a tmux session ends when its last client leaves, while the conversation runs on
in claude's background; see
.BR "SESSION LIFECYCLE" .
.PP
The conversation works a schedule: a GitHub issue of kind
.I chore
whose title begins
.IR Schedule: ,
its lane table listing the work.
.I SCHEDULE
is
.IR <owner>/<repository>#<number> ,
a bare
.I <number>
in the startup directory's repository, or
.BR new .
The issue is read with
.BR gh (1)
from its own repository, never the caller's, and an issue that is not a
schedule is refused.
.PP
.B "\-\-schedule new"
opens a schedule in the startup directory's repository. Its title is read from
standard input, with the prompt
.I "Schedule title:"
when standard input is a terminal, and it opens as
.IR "Schedule: <title>" ,
with an empty lane table. An empty title opens nothing. A title can be piped;
the tmux session then attaches through the terminal the launch runs in,
so a terminal is still needed, unless
.B \-\-detach
is given.
.PP
The conversation is handed its schedule:
.B NFEP_AGENT_SCHEDULE
holds
.IR <owner>/<repository>#<number> ,
and
.B NFEP_AGENT_SCHEDULE_URL
its link, in claude's background session, through
.BR "claude \-\-settings" ,
and in the shell pane. The viewer has no need of them.
.PP
When it is run from inside a tmux session it switches the client rather than
attempting to nest, and tmux ends the tmux session the client leaves when
Start-Claude opened it.
.PP
It starts in
.IR DIRECTORY ,
or else in the current directory, which must be the root of a repository clone
or of a linked worktree; otherwise it stops before anything is named or
started. Everything the conversation is named for is read there,
.BR gh (1)
lookups included, and
.B claude
starts there. The directory is resolved to its physical path first: a symlinked
path and its target are one repository but two project folders to Claude Code,
and neither could resume the other's conversations.
.SH SESSION NAMING
claude's background session carries the name
.PP
.nf
\&<owner>/<repository>#<number> | <host> | <repository> | <branch> | <title>
.fi
.PP
and each tmux session onto it the schedule's repository and number, with the
tmux session's own identifier after them, as Start-Claude.ps1 names a psmux
session, since psmux loses a session whose name carries / or |:
.PP
.nf
\&<repository>#<number> $<n>
.fi
.PP
A conversation on no schedule names its tmux session for its own identifier.
.PP
so that the
.B /resume
picker inside Claude Code, Remote Control and tmux show what the work is, and two
tmux sessions onto one conversation are told apart: tmux refuses a second session
of one name, and never reuses an identifier while its server runs. The name
claude carries is also the Remote Control session's, unless
.B \-\-no\-remote\-control
leaves Remote Control off.
.PP
The schedule comes first and in full: it is the conversation's key. Everything
after it is written afresh at each launch that starts the conversation, so a
retitled schedule, another host or another branch changes the name then.
.PP
.I <host>
is this box's short name, its domain dropped so the name never carries a dot.
A conversation resumes only on the box whose transcripts hold it, and Remote
Control lists every box's sessions together, so the name says which box runs
each.
.PP
.I <repository>
is the repository rather than the directory holding it, taken from
.IR remote.origin.url ,
then the toplevel directory, then the startup directory. A linked worktree is a
decorated
directory such as
.IR devlore\-cli.docker.devlore\-package ,
and naming it that way would make one repository look like several.
.PP
.I <title>
is the schedule's title, whole. No part of the name is cut.
.PP
A tmux session is never found again, by its name or otherwise: each start opens
its own, and every reference to one uses its identifier.
.B .
and
.B :
are legal in a session name but not in a tmux target, which is written
.IR session:window.pane ,
and issue titles carry colons constantly, so the name is kept verbatim.
.SH SESSION LIFECYCLE
Every conversation Start-Claude starts runs in claude's background service. The
store holds, for each schedule, the short identifier of the background session
that runs its conversation, as
.B "claude \-\-bg"
prints it and
.B "claude agents"
lists it: one file per schedule, under
.IR ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-~/.local/state}/agent\-schedule ,
its path the schedule's key. claude keeps that identifier across
.BR /clear ,
which gives the conversation a new one, so the store needs no updating as the
conversation goes on.
.PP
A start looks the stored identifier up in
.BR "claude agents \-\-json \-\-all" :
.IP \(bu 2
A background session of that identifier, running or stopped, is the schedule's,
and a new tmux session opens onto it:
.B "claude attach"
starts a stopped one again under the same identifier.
.IP \(bu 2
An entry can hold a conversation's identifier instead, as one written before its
conversation ran in claude's background does. The background session that runs
that conversation is the schedule's, and its identifier is stored. A
conversation that a claude runs in the foreground, which Start-Claude cannot
open, stops the script with status 75: the message names the host, the
process, its terminal and when it started, and says to end it there, with
.B /exit
or
.BR "kill \-TERM" ,
and run the same command again, and not to move it with
.BR /bg ,
which gives it a new identifier the store does not hold. A record whose process
is gone, left by a claude killed outright, is passed over. A conversation claude
runs nowhere is resumed in its background with
.BR "claude \-\-bg \-\-resume" ,
under its own identifier, which is stored.
.IP \(bu 2
With none stored, a new conversation starts in claude's background with
.BR "claude \-\-bg" ,
and the identifier it prints is stored.
.PP
A stored identifier that claude lists nowhere, and with no transcript here or
under another
.BR CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR ,
is set aside, and the schedule has none: a conversation that runs may have no
transcript yet. A schedule moved to another repository is followed there, and
its entry under the old key is reported, not carried over.
.PP
A conversation joins a schedule only through the store, never through its name:
.B \-\-resume
with
.B \-\-schedule
puts that conversation on a schedule that has none, or opens it on the schedule
whose conversation it is. A schedule is one conversation's, and a conversation
one schedule's: giving a schedule a second conversation, or a conversation a
second schedule, stops the script with status 73. The message names the entry to
remove to free the schedule. A conversation changed from inside Claude Code with
.B /resume
is not followed: the store keeps the background session the script started.
.PP
A schedule's conversation runs once, in claude's background, and is opened as
many times, from as many terminals and devices, as anyone likes, each time in a
tmux session of its own. The store keeps no tmux session, and none is found
again. Each has
.B destroy\-unattached
on, so tmux ends it when its last client leaves: at a detach, when its
connection ends or drops, or when its client switches to another tmux session.
Its shell pane ends with it, and whatever runs there. Start-Claude sets the
option once the client is attached, since on a tmux session no client has
attached, tmux ends it at once. A client that goes without a word keeps its tmux
session: one that
.BR mosh\-server (1)
holds for a client that is gone still counts as attached; see
.B MOSH_SERVER_NETWORK_TMOUT
under
.BR ENVIRONMENT .
.PP
From reading the schedule's entry to the moment its background session exists,
the script holds the store's lock, the kernel's lock on an empty file beside the
entry, taken with
.BR lockf (1)
on macOS and
.BR flock (1)
on Linux. A second launch of the schedule waits up to 10 seconds for it, then
finds the first's background session and opens a tmux session of its own onto
it. The lock ends when the script attaches or exits, however it exits, so none is
ever left behind. On any exit, the script removes a temporary store file, and a
tmux session it made but had not finished setting up.
.PP
.B \-\-resume
opens the conversation it names, as
.B "claude \-\-resume"
takes it but its picker: by its identifier, its background session's short
identifier, its name, or its transcript's absolute path.
.B \-\-continue
opens the conversation that
.B "claude agents \-\-json \-\-all"
lists in the startup directory, of several the one whose transcript was written
last, which claude's own
.B \-\-continue
would pick. With none listed,
.B \-\-continue
alone hands the startup directory to claude's own
.BR \-\-continue ,
which in claude's background starts a copy of the conversation it picks, under a
new identifier, and says so; given with
.BR \-\-schedule ,
it refuses and starts nothing, since claude's own pick goes by transcript and can
be another conversation. One of
.BR \-\-schedule ,
.B \-\-resume
and
.B \-\-continue
is given, or
.B \-\-resume
or
.B \-\-continue
with
.BR \-\-schedule .
A name is read only to find the conversation
.B \-\-resume
gives, never to choose a schedule. A conversation the store holds for a
schedule is that schedule's. A conversation the store does not hold is opened on
no schedule: claude's background session and Remote Control are named for its
identifier, nothing is stored, and a note says how to put it on one with
.B \-\-resume
and
.BR \-\-schedule .
.B \-\-continue
with none listed runs on no schedule too, named for the host, the repository and
the branch, and nothing is stored.
.PP
Leaving the viewer ends the tmux session: a
.B pane\-died
hook scoped to the viewer's pane, by
.BR #{hook_pane} ,
the pane the event is about, ends it, so a shell that exits on its own leaves the
workspace standing. The viewer's pane has
.B remain\-on\-exit
set, so it stays, dead, when the viewer exits, and the session with it until the
hook ends it: a pane that closed outright would take the session, and its hooks,
with it when it is the last.
.SH STARTING AND ATTACHING
Start-Claude attaches your terminal to the tmux session it opens, so how you
start it depends on whether you have one. There are two scenarios. Each command
line below works a schedule; give
.B \-\-resume
or
.B \-\-continue
in its place as you would anywhere.
.SS Interactive: a terminal must be available
Start-Claude attaches the terminal to a new tmux session. Leaving it, by leaving
the viewer or by detaching, ends the tmux session and returns you to the shell
that ran Start-Claude; claude runs the conversation on in its background.
.TP
.B Start-Claude \-\-schedule 916
In a terminal on the host, in the clone: the usual way.
.TP
.BI "ssh \-t " host " 'Start-Claude \-\-directory " clone " \-\-schedule 916'"
From another host. Start-Claude is the connection's command, so leaving the
tmux session ends the connection and returns you to your own shell.
.B ssh
gives a command a terminal only when
.B \-t
asks for one, and starts it in your home directory on
.IR host ,
so
.B \-\-directory
names the clone there. The command is quoted as one argument, so that no
.B ssh
client takes its options for its own, as Blink Shell's takes an unquoted
.BR \-\-directory .
.TP
.BI "mosh " host " \-\- Start-Claude \-\-directory " clone " \-\-schedule 916"
From another host, over a link that drops or changes, as a phone's does or a
sleeping laptop's. It works as it does over
.BR ssh ,
except that
.B mosh
reconnects on its own, and always gives its command a terminal.
.SS Non-interactive: no terminal is available
As from a script, cron, an iOS Shortcuts SSH action, or
.B ssh
without
.BR \-t .
None of the interactive command lines works there.
.TP
.BI "Start-Claude \-\-detach \-\-directory " clone " \-\-schedule 916"
Starts the conversation in claude's background if none runs, wakes it when
claude has stopped it, and opens no tmux session. The conversation is then
available on the host: reach it through Remote Control, under its name, or open
it later by running the same command in a terminal, without
.BR \-\-detach .
.PP
Without a terminal and without
.BR \-\-detach ,
Start-Claude starts nothing. It exits 69 and prints these command lines, filled
in with its own arguments, the startup directory's full path, and this host's
name as
.BR hostname (1)
gives it.
.SS On macOS: the login keychain
.BR gh (1)
and claude keep their logins in the login keychain, which an
.B ssh
session finds locked. Start-Claude checks it before it starts anything. Locked,
with a terminal, it runs
.BR "security unlock\-keychain login.keychain\-db" ,
which asks for your password. With no terminal,
.B \-\-detach
included, or when
.B security
fails to unlock it, Start-Claude starts nothing, exits 69, and prints the
interactive command lines above, filled in the same way. On macOS, then, a start
with no terminal works only where the keychain is already open.
.SS claude's trust in the folder
claude starts nothing in its background in a folder it does not trust. It says
what to do instead, which is to run
.B claude
there once and accept its trust question, and Start-Claude passes its words
through, exits 69, and opens nothing. Trusting a folder is your decision, made in
claude; Start-Claude reads and records nothing of it.
.PP
If the other host does not find Start-Claude, give its absolute path on that
host, as
.B "command \-v Start-Claude"
prints it there. Over
.BR ssh ,
your own shell would expand a
.B ~
before ssh sends it, and
.B mosh
runs its command with no shell to expand one.
.SH EXITING AND DETACHING
Leaving a tmux session and stopping the conversation are different things.
.PP
To
.B leave the tmux session
leave the viewer, with Escape or Ctrl+Z, or detach from tmux: the tmux prefix,
then
.BR d .
The prefix is
.B C\-b
unless you have rebound it; see
.BR tmux (1).
Either way the tmux session ends, its shell pane with it, and you are returned to
the shell you started from, while claude runs the conversation on in its
background: the same command opens a new tmux session onto it, from this
terminal or another.
.B /exit
in a viewer does nothing.
.PP
To
.B stop the conversation
stop its background session with
.BR "claude stop" ,
given the identifier the store holds. claude keeps the conversation: the next
start opens a tmux session onto it, and the viewer starts it again.
.SS The prefix collides with Claude Code's own Ctrl+B
Claude Code binds Ctrl+B to
.IR "run in background" ,
and at the default prefix tmux consumes that keystroke before claude sees it.
.PP
Claude Code detects this. It reads the prefix at startup and, when it is one of
.BR "C-b C-c C-d C-t C-o C-r C-s C-g C-e" ,
changes its own hint to read
.I "ctrl+b ctrl+b (twice)"
instead of
.IR ctrl+b .
Pressing the prefix twice works because tmux binds
.B C\-b
under the prefix to
.BR send\-prefix ,
which passes one through to the pane.
.PP
So nothing is lost, but the keystroke is doubled. Rebinding the tmux prefix to
something outside that list \(em
.B C\-a
is the usual choice \(em gives Ctrl+B back to claude at the cost of the
convention every other tmux user shares.
.PP
.TS
tab(;);
l l l.
;\fBLeave the viewer\fR;\fBDetach\fR
claude's background session;runs on;runs on
The tmux session;ends;ends
Where you land;your shell;your shell
Re\-running the script;a new tmux session onto it;a new tmux session onto it
.TE
.PP
Either way you land at the shell you started from rather than in another tmux
session, because
.B detach\-on\-destroy
is
.B on
by default. With several sessions open that is worth knowing, since switching
to
one of the others is the plausible alternative.
.PP
Started as the command of an
.B ssh
or
.B mosh
connection, the shell you started from is your own, on your own host; see
.BR "STARTING AND ATTACHING" .
.SH THE SHELL PANE
The right pane runs your default shell in the startup directory, with the
schedule's
.B NFEP_AGENT_SCHEDULE
and
.B NFEP_AGENT_SCHEDULE_URL
in its environment. Nothing is sent to it and nothing
is started in it.
.PP
Earlier revisions tailed a log there: a file named with
.B \-\-log
or the first of
.IR app.log ,
.IR development.log ,
.IR output.log ,
or
.I server.log
found in the launch directory. Both the option and the search are gone. A pane
whose purpose is to
run something already chosen is a pane that has spent the prompt it could have
offered, and a
.BR tail (1)
is one command to type in a shell that is now there to take it.
.PP
Exiting the shell closes only that pane and leaves the viewer open; see
.B SESSION LIFECYCLE
for why the tmux session ends with the viewer's pane and not with this one.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-continue
Open the conversation claude lists in the startup directory, as
.B "claude agents \-\-json \-\-all"
lists it, of several the one whose transcript was written last; with none
listed, hand the directory to claude's own
.BR \-\-continue ,
which in claude's background starts a copy of the conversation it picks, and
says so. With
.BR \-\-schedule ,
put the conversation it opens on that schedule, and with none listed, refuse
and start nothing. See
.BR "SESSION LIFECYCLE" .
.TP
.BI \-C " DIRECTORY" "\fR, \fP" \-\-directory " DIRECTORY"
Start in
.IR DIRECTORY ,
the root of a repository clone or of a linked worktree, instead of the current
directory. See
.BR DESCRIPTION .
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-detach
Start the conversation in claude's background, or wake it under its name when
claude has stopped it, and open no tmux session. Start-Claude exits 0, with a
terminal or without, and the conversation is available on this host: reach it
through Remote Control under its name, or open it later by running the same
command without
.BR \-\-detach .
After
.BR "\-\-schedule new" ,
which that command would run again, Start-Claude prints the command to run
instead, naming the schedule it opened. Remote Control needs claude signed in.
Not with
.BR \-\-no\-remote\-control .
See
.BR "STARTING AND ATTACHING" .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print this page, or the synopsis when it is unavailable, and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-remote\-control
Start claude's background session without Remote Control. Without it, the
session starts with
.BR \-\-remote\-control ,
named for the conversation. A background session that runs already keeps the
options it was started with, and the option changes nothing there. Not with
.BR \-\-detach .
.TP
.BI \-r " CONVERSATION" "\fR, \fP" \-\-resume " CONVERSATION"
Open the conversation
.I CONVERSATION
names, as
.B "claude \-\-resume"
takes it but its picker: its identifier, a UUID, that claude lists or a
transcript in any project folder holds; its background session's short
identifier, as
.B "claude agents \-\-json \-\-all"
lists it; its name, matched whole against the names claude lists for its
background sessions and the last name each transcript records; or its
transcript's absolute path. Its background session is opened, or, when claude
runs it nowhere, the conversation is resumed in claude's background under its
own identifier. With
.BR \-\-schedule ,
put it on that schedule. See
.BR "SESSION LIFECYCLE" .
.TP
.BI \-s " SCHEDULE" "\fR, \fP" \-\-schedule " SCHEDULE"
The schedule the conversation works:
.IR <owner>/<repository>#<number> ,
a bare
.I <number>
in the startup directory's repository, or
.BR new .
See
.BR DESCRIPTION .
.TP
.BI \-w " PERCENT" "\fR, \fP" \-\-shell\-width\-percent " PERCENT"
Width of the right\-hand pane as a percentage of the window, between 1 and 99
(default: 40).
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I claude-argument
Everything after
.B \-\-
is appended to the
.B "claude \-\-bg"
command line that starts a conversation in claude's background, after the flags
the script supplies, and the background session keeps it. A background session
that runs already keeps its own, and Start-Claude says so. Arguments reach
.B claude
through
.BR execve (2)
with no intervening shell, so quoting is preserved: an argument containing
spaces arrives as
one argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR
Where Claude Code keeps its configuration and its transcripts;
.I ~/.claude
when unset. The script looks in its
.I projects
directory for a conversation's transcript.
.TP
.B HOME
Base for
.I ~/.claude
when
.B CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR
is unset, and for
.I ~/.local/state
when
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
is unset.
.TP
.B HOSTNAME
Its first label names the host in the conversation's name, and the command lines
a launch with no terminal prints name the host by it whole;
.BR hostname (1)
is asked when it is unset.
.TP
.B MOSH_SERVER_NETWORK_TMOUT
Not read by the script:
.BR mosh\-server (1)
reads it as it starts, so it is set, if at all, where the shell that ssh starts
for mosh reads it, such as
.IR ~/.zshenv .
Unset, mosh\-server waits for its client indefinitely, so a client that goes
without a word, as when Blink Shell is quit, leaves the tmux session it attached
in place, its client still counted as attached. Set, mosh\-server exits that
many seconds after its client was last heard from; the tmux session loses its
last client, and tmux ends it. The conversation runs on in claude's background
either way. Whether to set it, and to what, is the user's choice.
.TP
.B NFEP_AGENT_SCHEDULE
Set to the conversation's schedule,
.IR <owner>/<repository>#<number> ,
in claude's background session, through
.BR "claude \-\-settings" ,
and in the tmux session's shell pane.
.TP
.B NFEP_AGENT_SCHEDULE_URL
Set to the schedule's link, as
.B NFEP_AGENT_SCHEDULE
is set to the schedule.
.TP
.B TMUX
When set, the script switches the current client to the new tmux session instead
of attaching, and a launch counts as having a terminal. tmux then ends the tmux
session the client left when Start-Claude opened it.
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Where the store lives, in
.I agent\-schedule
under it;
.I ~/.local/state
when unset.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-~/.claude}/projects
Checked for the transcript of the conversation the store holds or
.B \-\-resume
names, in whichever project folder holds it, and, for
.BR \-\-continue ,
for which listed conversation's transcript was written last. Nothing else of
claude's is read.
.TP
.I ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-~/.local/state}/agent\-schedule/<owner>/<repository>/<number>
The store's entry for a schedule. Its one line is the short identifier of the
background session that runs the schedule's conversation, such as
.BR 24b62a42 ,
or a conversation's identifier until a start puts that conversation in claude's
background: the one thing that cannot be computed. Its path is the schedule's
key. Written through a temporary file and a rename, and never deleted by the
script.
.TP
.I ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-~/.local/state}/agent\-schedule/<owner>/<repository>/<number>.lock
The store's lock for the schedule: an empty file, locked while a launch
reads the
store and starts the background session. Kept, and never read.
.TP
.I ~/.local/libexec/agent\-schedule
This platform's
.I lock\-store.sh
and
.IR keychain.sh ,
off the PATH, at
.I ../libexec/agent\-schedule
from the directory that holds Start-Claude: the store's lock, with
.BR lockf (1)
on macOS and
.BR flock (1)
on Linux, and the login keychain's check, which on Linux finds nothing to unlock.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success: the background session's short id is on standard output; with
.BR \-\-detach ,
claude runs the conversation in its background, available on this host.
.TP
.B 64
Usage error: an unrecognized option; none of
.BR \-\-schedule ,
.B \-\-resume
and
.BR \-\-continue ,
or
.B \-\-continue
with
.BR \-\-resume ;
a malformed
.BR \-\-schedule ;
a
.B \-\-shell\-width\-percent
outside 1\-99; or
.B \-\-detach
with
.BR \-\-no\-remote\-control .
.TP
.B 65
The schedule's issue is not a schedule;
.B "\-\-schedule new"
read an empty title; several conversations carry the name
.B \-\-resume
gives, or the transcript it names is not named for a conversation's identifier;
claude's list of its sessions is not the JSON the script reads; or claude's words
on a background start name no background session the script reads.
.TP
.B 66
The startup directory does not exist or is not the root of a repository clone,
its origin is not on GitHub where a bare number or
.B new
needs it, or the schedule's issue could not be read, the message ending with
gh's own; or nothing answers to what
.B \-\-resume
gives: no session claude lists by that identifier and no transcript of it, no
background session by that short identifier, no conversation by that name, or no
transcript at that path; or claude lists no conversation in the startup
directory for
.B \-\-continue
with
.BR \-\-schedule .
.TP
.B 69
.BR claude ,
.BR gh (1),
.BR jq (1)
or
.BR tmux (1)
is not installed; this platform's
.I lock\-store.sh
or
.I keychain.sh
is not in
.I ../libexec/agent\-schedule
from the script's directory;
.B "claude agents \-\-json \-\-all"
failed; claude started nothing in its background, as in a folder it does not
trust, the message ending with claude's own words; claude did not wake a stopped
background session; there is no terminal to attach the tmux session to, and
.B \-\-detach
was not given; or, on macOS, the login keychain is locked and there is no
terminal to unlock it from, or
.B security
failed to unlock it. Each of the last two starts nothing and prints the command
lines that start it: see
.BR "STARTING AND ATTACHING" .
.TP
.B 73
.B "\-\-schedule new"
could not open the schedule, the message ending with gh's own; the conversation
is another schedule's; the schedule already has another conversation; or the
store cannot be written.
.TP
.B 74
The store's entry for the schedule could not be read, or holds neither a
background session's identifier nor a conversation's.
.TP
.B 75
Another launch held the store's lock for 10 seconds, the message naming the
schedule; or a claude runs the conversation in the foreground, the message
naming it and how to move the conversation into claude's background whole.
.TP
.B 78
